22:04:53 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I see the logic you’re using, and it’s a really intuitive way to think about it. You’re essentially saying: *electricity is ultimately a cheaper form of energy than fossil fuels, so why wouldn’t it win in every application?* Let’s dig into where that assumption hits a snag. ### The crucial missing piece: Delivered cost per unit of heat You’re right that charging a phone or running an electric motor is extremely cheap compared to filling a gas can—but **the comparison isn’t between “electricity production” and “gasoline production” in a vacuum. It’s about how much *usable energy* you get for your dollar at the point of use.** Here’s the counterintuitive part: at the consumer level, a unit of energy delivered as electricity usually costs **3–5 times more** than the same unit of energy delivered as natural gas. Yes, you read that right. - Residential electricity: ~$0.15 per kWh, which translates to about **$44 per million BTU**. - Residential natural gas: ~$1.50 per therm, which is about **$15 per million BTU**. So in raw energy terms, electricity is actually *more expensive* to buy than gas for many people. That’s not a mistake—it reflects the losses and infrastructure costs of making and moving electricity. ### Why does electricity cost so much per BTU? Because when you burn gas (or coal, or use nuclear) to make electricity, you lose a huge chunk of the original energy as waste heat: - A modern combined-cycle power plant might be 60% efficient at best. When you add transmission and distribution losses (~5–8%), only about half the original fuel energy reaches your home as usable electricity. - The gas you burn in a furnace? That’s burned right where you need the heat, at 90–98% efficiency, with nearly no transport losses. So even if the raw natural gas *commodity* is cheap, turning it into electricity and bringing it to your outlet makes it an expensive way to get a pure BTU. That’s why plugging in a space heater often costs more than running the gas furnace. ### Why does the same expensive electricity win for cars? Only because the **application** is completely different. A gasoline engine is terribly inefficient—only about 20–30% of the fuel energy makes it to the wheels, the rest is waste heat. An electric motor, on the other hand, is 85–90% efficient. So even though you’re paying more per unit of energy for electricity, you need so much less of it to travel the same mile that you come out ahead. That efficiency multiplier is massive. In heating, a simple electric resistance heater gives you **no efficiency multiplier**—it’s 100% efficient at turning expensive electricity into heat, but a gas furnace is also 90–98% efficient at turning cheap gas into heat. The “multiplier” is literally 1-to-1 for electricity, while the gas path has a tiny loss. The fuel cost per usable BTU is what decides the winner, and here, gas often wins. ### What about the “cheaper to produce” idea? You might be thinking of renewable electricity—wind and solar are incredibly cheap to generate once built. And indeed, in places with abundant renewable energy, wholesale electricity prices can drop to near zero. But that’s not the price consumers pay. The retail price includes grid maintenance, backup generation for when the sun isn’t shining, transmission lines, taxes, etc. So the delivered cost remains high, and the BTU comparison still holds in most markets. ### The real home-heating equivalent of an EV It’s not a space heater—it’s a **heat pump**. A heat pump doesn’t create heat; it moves it. For every unit of expensive electricity you put in, you can get 2–4 units of heat out (depending on outdoor temperature). That efficiency multiplier finally overcomes the high cost of electrical BTUs and can beat gas on running cost. That’s why policy and incentives are pushing heat pumps so hard right now—they’re the technology that makes the EV-like economic case for home heating. So your core intuition—that electricity is “cheaper” —isn’t wrong in principle; it just applies at the wholesale generation level for certain renewables, not at your wall socket when compared to directly burning gas for heat. The efficiency multiplier is the missing link that makes EVs a win, and heat pumps are finally bringing that same logic to your living room.
