tric heating is cheaper than *all* gas heating. It says that her specific upgrade — almost certainly a heat pump — was the right economic move for her situation. ### What this proves about the EV comparison Your cousin’s success actually strengthens the parallel: just as an EV’s efficiency makes it cheaper to drive despite electricity’s high cost per unit of energy, a heat pump’s efficiency makes it cheaper to heat a home despite the same issue. The technology that “breaks the BTU price barrier” is the heat pump, not the electric wire itself. So yes, a lot of people can and do save money by switching to electric heating — but it’s because they’re adopting the technology that works *like* an electric car, not because any electric heater will do. That’s why incentives and building codes are increasingly pushing for heat pumps, and why you’ll hear more stories like your cousin’s. They’re not counterexamples; they’re the leading edge of the exact same transition we’re seeing on the roads.
