s rates, a resistive electric system is almost always the most expensive way to heat your home. Builders don’t use it as the primary heat source in cold climates for exactly that reason, unless the house is super-insulated to passive house standards. But here’s the key: **you almost never hear stories about people installing electric baseboard heat as an “upgrade” and saving money**, because it typically doesn’t happen. Those outcomes don’t make for happy anecdotes. The narrative you encounter is shaped by the successful cases—which almost universally involve heat pumps. ### The EV parallel, completed Saying “going electric saves you money everywhere” without specifying the technology is a bit like saying “going electric with your car saves you money” but forgetting to mention that the car has a battery and motor, not a set of jumper cables hooked to a toaster oven on wheels. The specific technology matters enormously. A heat pump is the electric car of heating; a resistive strip is an electric toaster that happens to sit in your ductwork. ### So am I the only one making this “complicated exception”? Not at all. Every utility rebate program, energy audit, and building efficiency standard makes this exact distinction. They don’t hand out subsidies for electric baseboard heaters; they hand them out for heat pumps. The Department of Energy’s whole push for electrification is centered on heat pumps, because they know that straight resistive electric heating would spike grid demand and consumer bills. The reason it feels like I’m the only one saying it is that the public conversation often simplifies to “electric = good, gas = bad,” and that slogan works because the technology leading the charge—heat pumps—makes it true in practice for a growing number of people. I’m just adding the asterisk that explains why it works: it’s the heat pump, not the electric wire. So you’re not wrong to trust what your friends are showing you. Their savings are real. I’m just shining a light on the mechanism behind their success, and why a different electric heating choice (or a different location) would give a very different result. That’s the difference between a general rule of thumb and a universal law.
